{
  "section": "interview-questions",
  "slug": "key-value-store",
  "group": "medium",
  "no": "QUESTION // 17",
  "tag": "MEDIUM",
  "access": "paid",
  "date": "6 Nov 2026",
  "title": "Design a distributed key-value store",
  "dek": "Zotato keeps carts, sessions and app settings for 20 crore accounts: about a terabyte of small values, read lakhs of times a second. Build a store that spreads them over many machines, keeps three copies, and keeps working when a machine dies.",
  "body": [
    {
      "t": "example"
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "1. Requirements"
    },
    {
      "t": "list",
      "items": [
        "get(key), put(key, value), delete(key). Values are small, up to a few KB.",
        "Keep working when a machine, or a whole zone, is down.",
        "Grow by adding machines, without stopping or moving most of the data.",
        "Out of scope here: queries by anything other than the key, and transactions across keys."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "table",
      "label": "Qualities to aim for",
      "head": [
        "Quality",
        "Target"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Speed",
          "Reads and writes in a few milliseconds"
        ],
        [
          "Durability",
          "A confirmed write survives any one machine dying"
        ],
        [
          "Availability",
          "Keeps answering with one zone of three down"
        ],
        [
          "Scale",
          "20 crore accounts, about 1 TB, 2 lakh reads and 20,000 writes a second"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "2. The numbers that matter"
    },
    {
      "t": "list",
      "items": [
        "Data: 20 crore accounts × about 5 KB of carts, sessions and settings is 1 TB. With three copies, 3 TB.",
        "If each machine holds about 250 GB comfortably, that's 12 machines; run 15 to 20 so each has room to grow and to take a dead neighbour's share.",
        "Reads: 2 lakh a second across 15 machines is 4 lakh copy-reads with R = 2, about 27,000 each, fine for keys that are mostly in memory.",
        "Writes: 20,000 a second, each sent to three copies, is 60,000 copy-writes a second across the cluster, about 4,000 per machine."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "3. Entities and API"
    },
    {
      "t": "list",
      "items": [
        "Value: `key`, `value` (bytes), `version` (a version vector: one counter per machine that wrote it, so two writes made apart, say on both sides of a network split, show up as concurrent; both are kept and the reader, here the cart service, merges them), `deleted` (a marker, so a delete isn't undone by an old copy).",
        "Ring: which machines own which slices of the hash range, shared by every node and client library."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "code",
      "label": "API",
      "x": "GET    /kv/cart:u_42          → 200 { \"value\": \"…\", \"version\": 8 }\nPUT    /kv/cart:u_42          { \"value\": \"…\", \"if_version\": 8 }   → 200 { \"version\": 9 }\nDELETE /kv/cart:u_42          → 204"
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "4. High-level design"
    },
    {
      "t": "fig",
      "id": "ring",
      "cap": "Machines and keys are hashed onto a ring. cart:u_42 lands between A and B, so it's stored on the next three machines clockwise: B, C and D. When E joins, it takes over only the slice next to it."
    },
    {
      "t": "p",
      "x": "Any machine can take a request: it works out the key's owners from the ring and talks to them. Clients can also do this themselves and go straight to an owner, saving a hop."
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "5. Deep dives"
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "Where a key lives"
    },
    {
      "t": "type",
      "name": "First try",
      "kind": "HASH MOD THE NUMBER OF MACHINES",
      "what": "Machine = hash(key) mod N.",
      "use": "Even spread, one line of code.",
      "avoid": "Go from 15 machines to 16 and almost every key's machine changes. Adding one machine means moving nearly the whole terabyte.",
      "ex": "Adding a machine before Diwali sends 94% of carts to new homes, while customers are shopping.",
      "like": "Renumbering every house in the street because one new house was built."
    },
    {
      "t": "type",
      "name": "Better",
      "kind": "CONSISTENT HASHING WITH VIRTUAL NODES",
      "what": "Hash machines onto a ring of values, many times each (virtual nodes). A key belongs to the next three different machines clockwise from its hash, skipping extra points of a machine already picked.",
      "use": "Adding a machine moves only about 1/N of the keys, taken a little from every machine; losing one spreads its load over everyone.",
      "avoid": "Every node and client needs the same ring. Keep it in a small consensus-backed store (see the consensus article) and cache it.",
      "ex": "Going from 15 to 16 machines moves about 6% of keys, a little from each of the 15.",
      "like": "A new house gets a number between its neighbours; nobody else's address changes."
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "Three copies that agree enough"
    },
    {
      "t": "fig",
      "id": "quorum",
      "cap": "A write waits for two of three copies; C was slow and missed it. A read asks two, B and C: B has version 8, so 8 is returned and C is repaired. With 2 + 2 > 3, every read overlaps the write."
    },
    {
      "t": "list",
      "items": [
        "N copies, W to confirm a write, R to answer a read. With N = 3, W = 2, R = 2, reads see the latest confirmed write.",
        "Choose by need: carts use W = 2, R = 2; a \"last seen\" timestamp could use W = 1, R = 1 for speed, accepting a stale read now and then.",
        "Put the three copies in three different zones, so losing a zone loses one copy, not two.",
        "Two writers at once: give each value a version and write with \"only if still version 8\". This needs one decider per key (a leader per slice, see follow-ups); without it, two writers can each land on some copies."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "When machines fail"
    },
    {
      "t": "list",
      "items": [
        "Machines tell each other who is up (gossip, or heartbeats to a coordinator). A machine that misses several is treated as down.",
        "While a copy's owner is down, another machine takes its writes and hands them over when it's back. Until then, a read of two owners can miss that write, so 2 + 2 > 3 only holds if a write counts only owners' confirmations.",
        "Copies drift (a missed write, a disk error). A background job compares copies range by range, using a tree of hashes of each range (a Merkle tree) so only differing ranges are sent, and fixes them.",
        "A dead machine for good: its slices are copied from the surviving copies to new owners, from many machines at once."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "On one machine"
    },
    {
      "t": "list",
      "items": [
        "A write is appended to a log on disk (flushed before confirming, so a crash loses nothing), then put in an in-memory table.",
        "When the in-memory table fills, it's written out as a sorted file. Reads check memory, then the newest files first; a small filter per file skips files that can't have the key.",
        "Files are merged in the background (compaction), dropping old versions and deleted keys after a while. This shape is called an LSM tree."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "h2",
      "x": "What's expected at each level"
    },
    {
      "t": "list",
      "items": [
        "Junior / mid: get/put by key, partitioning across machines, copies for safety, a cache-like in-memory read path.",
        "Senior: consistent hashing and virtual nodes, N/W/R and what they buy, versions and conditional writes, zones.",
        "Lead: failure detection and handing writes over, repairing drift, adding and removing machines live, the LSM write and read path."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "more",
      "label": "Follow-ups an interviewer may ask",
      "body": [
        {
          "t": "list",
          "items": [
            "\"One key gets very hot?\" Cache it in front, or for counters split it into several keys and add them up (see the like counter in the photo-sharing question).",
            "\"Why not just use a database?\" For this shape of data, a key-value store scales out more simply; a relational database is better when you need queries and transactions.",
            "\"Deletes come back to life?\" That's why deletes are written as markers with a version and only cleaned up after every copy has seen them.",
            "\"Strong consistency for some keys?\" Use a leader per slice chosen by consensus, and send those keys' reads and writes to it, at the cost of some speed.",
            "\"How do you test it?\" Kill machines and cut network links during load, then check that every confirmed write can still be read, with the right version."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "lab",
      "x": "A confirmed write must survive a crash. The lost-orders challenge is about exactly that promise.",
      "to": "problems/challenge/lost-orders",
      "go": "Try the lost-orders challenge"
    }
  ],
