            "concurrent write"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "nfr": {
        "availability": [
          99.9,
          99.999
        ],
        "p99": [
          5,
          200
        ],
        "consistency": "eventual",
        "readsPerWrite": [
          3,
          30
        ]
      },
      "est": [
        {
          "id": "data",
          "label": "Total data (one copy)",
          "unit": "TB",
          "answer": 1,
          "tol": 3,
          "how": "20 crore accounts × 5 KB"
        },
        {
          "id": "machines",
          "label": "Machines to hold three copies",
          "unit": "machines",
          "answer": 12,
          "tol": 3,
          "how": "3 TB ÷ 250 GB each"
        },
        {
          "id": "copywrites",
          "label": "Copy-writes a second",
          "unit": "/s",
          "answer": 60000,
          "tol": 3,
          "how": "20,000 writes × 3 copies"
        }
      ],
      "api": [
        {
          "label": "Get a key",
          "method": "GET",
          "path": "^/(api/)?(v\\d+/)?(kv|keys|store)/[^/]+"
        },
        {
          "label": "Put a key",
          "method": "PUT|POST",
          "path": "^/(api/)?(v\\d+/)?(kv|keys|store)/[^/]+"
        },
        {
          "label": "Delete a key",
          "method": "DELETE",
          "path": "^/(api/)?(v\\d+/)?(kv|keys|store)/[^/]+"
        }
      ],
      "decisions": [
        {
          "id": "place-2",
          "q": "How do you decide which machine holds a key?",
          "options": [
            "The key's hash modulo the number of machines",
            "Consistent hashing: a ring of virtual nodes",
            "One central table that lists every key's machine",
            "A random machine, then ask all of them on reads"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "why": "With modulo N, adding one machine moves almost every key; on a ring only the keys next to the new machine move. A central table is a bottleneck and a single point of failure, and asking every machine doesn't scale."
        },
        {
          "id": "quorum-2",
          "q": "Each key is kept on 3 machines. How many must answer a write and a read so the read sees the write?",
          "options": [
            "1 for writes and 1 for reads",
            "3 for writes and 1 for reads",
            "2 for writes and 2 for reads"
          ],
          "answer": 2,
          "why": "When W + R is more than N, every read overlaps at least one copy that has the latest write. W = 2, R = 2 keeps working with one machine down; W = 3 fails writes whenever any copy is down, and 1 + 1 can read a stale copy."
        },
        {
          "id": "conflict-2",
          "q": "Two clients wrote the same key on both sides of a network split. What then?",
          "options": [
            "Keep both versions; the reader merges them",
            "Keep the write with the later server clock",
            "Refuse every write until the split is over"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Clocks on different machines disagree, so \"last write wins\" can throw away the newer write without anyone knowing. Version vectors show the two writes were concurrent and keep both; refusing writes gives up availability."
        }
      ]
    },
    "hints": [
      {
        "step": "requirements",
        "x": "A machine dies, a zone dies, you add machines. What must still be true each time?"
      },
      {
        "step": "estimates",
        "x": "Data size × 3 copies ÷ what one machine holds gives the machine count."
      },
      {
        "step": "api",
        "x": "GET, PUT (with an optional expected version), DELETE on a key."
      },
      {
        "step": "design",
        "x": "Hot keys answered from memory: a cache in front of the storage nodes takes most reads, because a few keys are read far more than the rest."
      },
      {
        "step": "design",
        "x": "60,000 a second is more than one storage node: several databases share the keys (the ring)."
      },
      {
        "step": "design",
        "x": "Copies in other zones (Multi-AZ) so a confirmed write survives a dead machine."
      }
    ]
